WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.029
There's a lot that goes into movie-making,
especially huge productions like the Marvel

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:07.930
Studios mega-blockbuster Avengers: Infinity
War.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:12.269
Between principal photography, adding CGI
effects, and reshoots, scheduling can become

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:14.809
a mind-bogglingly complex equation.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:19.630
Often, studios like Marvel have to begin editing
before they've even finished filming the movie.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.230
So it's no wonder that mistakes start to creep
in.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:26.320
And despite how awesome Avengers: Infinity
War is, even the biggest blockbuster event

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.810
of the year isn't immune to editing mistakes
- as one eagle-eyed Marvel fan discovered

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:35.810
when he found a glaring continuity error that
somehow completely slipped past everyone at

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.160
Marvel Studios.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.480
What is this huge mistake?

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.610
Well, during the climactic battle at the end
of the film, Thanos is shown grabbing Vision

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.990
by the throat on two separate occasions.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.880
Everything you ever knew is a lie!

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.320
Okay, so maybe it's not that big a deal.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:55.730
In fact, you probably didn't even notice it
when you were watching Avengers: Infinity

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:56.810
War in the theater.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:01:01.760
But once the mistake was pointed out by Reddit
user FoorAJ in a handy graphic, it's pretty

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.760
hard to miss.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.100
The scene in question takes place just after
Scarlet Witch uses her hex power to destroy

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.450
Vision's Mind Stone, preventing Thanos from
completing the Infinity Gauntlet and destroying

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.499
half the life in the universe.

00:01:13.499 --> 00:01:18.700
Unfortunately, Thanos uses the Time Stone
to rewind history, and once Vision is alive

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.750
again, he grabs the synthezoid by the throat.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.959
When Scarlet Witch tries to intervene, Thanos
clobbers her.

00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:29.211
After she hits the ground, the camera pans
back over to Thanos and Vision and Thanos

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:33.810
is shown reaching for Vision's throat even
though he just had his big, meaty villain

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.029
hand around it a few seconds before.

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:40.560
Thanos then lifts Vision off the ground, holds
him in the air by his throat, and scoops out

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.560
the Mind Stone.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.759
We know Thanos' whole "thing" is taking tyranny
to the extreme and being as over-the-top as

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:50.840
possible not quite as dramatic as the late
Loki but pretty close but a double chokehold

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.249
seems a bit excessive.

00:01:52.249 --> 00:01:56.039
Though it's definitely detectible and certainly
won't go unnoticed now that it's made the

00:01:56.039 --> 00:01:59.829
rounds on the internet, this mistake doesn't
really affect the overall quality of the movie

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:00.869
at all.

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:05.610
Even after learning Thanos grabs Vision by
the throat twice in the span of a few seconds,

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.819
every Marvel fan would likely say that Avengers:
Infinity War is still as action-packed, at

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:14.540
times hilarious, and ultimately heart-wrenching
as it was before anyone noticed that small

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.540
editing mistake.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.400
And more than that, this certainly isn't the
first time a Marvel Cinematic Universe installment

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.189
has featured an error, or at least a perceived
error.

00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:26.730
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, for instance, a
graphic stated that the film took place eight

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.840
years after the events of the first Avengers
movie, when it should have only been 5.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.780
And remember the mysterious case of Bucky
Barnes' changing birthday?

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.340
His birth year has been variously listed as
1916, 1917, and 1922.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.230
And there was the car dent that fixed itself
in The Avengers, the money that magically

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.610
flew out of a wallet in The Incredible Hulk,
and the disappearing headset in Iron Man.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.269
And… well, we could go on and on, but why
nitpick some of our favorite films?

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:57.409
If anything, these little harmless goofups
are just reassuring proof that while the Avengers

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:01.110
may be superhuman, the folks at Marvel who
make these great films are just normal people

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:05.549
like the rest of us - people who sometimes
make mistakes, and sometimes make great movies.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:10.070
It just makes us more excited than ever to
find out what happens in Avengers 4, which

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.210
is scheduled to arrive in theaters in May,
2019.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.849
Will the dead return to life?

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:16.079
Will the Avengers prevail?

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:18.670
Will Thanos choke Vision a third time?!

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.930
Whatever happens, we can't wait to find out.

